.TH TAKEGAWA 1 "2026-11-07"
.SH NAME
takegawa - TGPM, the MochiBerry package manager

.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY takegawa
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ].\|.\|.\&
.RI [ OPTION ].\|.\|.
.YS

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBtakegawa\fR is TGPM, the package manager for MochiBerry.
It is a separate tool from the engine and runs on it.
.PP
TGPM keeps packages in a single global, content-addressed store.
Each package tree is hashed with SHA-256 and stored once; projects refer to the
stored copy through a link (symlink or, on Windows, a directory junction).
Installing the same package in many projects therefore consumes store space
once instead of duplicating it in every project.
.PP
A copy mode is available for tools that expect a real
\fBnode_modules\fR directory, and an existing \fBnode_modules\fR tree is also
understood when resolving dependencies.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install
Install the dependencies declared in \fBtakegawa.json\fR (or
\fBpackage.json\fR). With an argument, behaves like \fBadd\fR.

.TP
.BI add " PKG[@RANGE]"
Add a dependency to the manifest, resolve it and install it.

.TP
.B init
Create a minimal \fBtakegawa.json\fR (and \fBindex.js\fR if missing) in the
current directory when no manifest exists.

.TP
.BI fetch " URL|FILE.tgz"
Download (http/https) or read a local tarball, cache it under
\fB$TAKEGAWA_HOME/cache\fR by content hash, extract it and print the
resolved package. Remote specs may also be passed directly to \fBadd\fR or
\fBinstall\fR, and a local package directory can be added with a
\fBfile:\fR spec.

.TP
.BI exec " CMD [ARGS...]"
Run a command with the project \fB.bin\fR directory and the global bin
directory prepended to \fBPATH\fR, so package binaries resolve.

.TP
.BI remove " PKG"
Remove a dependency from the manifest and unlink it from the project.

.TP
.B update
Ignore the lockfile and re-resolve every dependency against the newest
versions the manifest ranges allow.

.TP
.B resolve
Print the resolved dependency tree without installing.

.TP
.B outdated
List direct dependencies whose locked version is older than the newest
version allowed by the manifest range.

.TP
.BI info " PKG[@RANGE]"
Print the resolved metadata of a package: version, source, entry point,
active export conditions and runtime dependencies.

.TP
.B verify
Re-hash the store entries referenced by \fBtakegawa.lock\fR and report
integrity mismatches.

.TP
.B gc
Remove store entries that no lockfile under the current directory (or the
given root) and no globally installed package reference. Use
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR to preview.

.TP
.B link
Link the current package into the global store so other packages can use it.

.TP
.B pack
Pack the current package into a \fB.tgz\fR archive.

.TP
.BI run " SCRIPT"
Run a script from the manifest's \fBscripts\fR object.

.TP
.B store
Print the global store, cache, global and registry locations.

.TP
.B doctor
Check the environment, the manifest and filesystem capabilities.

.TP
.B version
Print the TGPM version.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-global
Operate on the global store.

.TP
.B \-\-node\-modules, \-\-copy
Copy packages into \fBnode_modules\fR instead of linking them.

.TP
.B \-\-production
Skip \fBdevDependencies\fR.

.TP
.B \-\-frozen\-lockfile
Fail instead of updating \fBtakegawa.lock\fR; use in CI to guarantee a
reproducible install.

.TP
.B \-\-update
Ignore the lockfile and re-resolve (same as the \fBupdate\fR command).

.TP
.BI \-\-registry " DIR"
Use \fIDIR\fR as the package registry instead of \fB$TAKEGAWA_HOME/registry\fR.

.TP
.BI \-\-home " DIR"
Override \fB$TAKEGAWA_HOME\fR (default \fB~/.takegawa\fR).

.SH THE MANIFEST
The manifest is \fBtakegawa.json\fR (a \fBpackage.json\fR with a \fBname\fR and
\fBversion\fR is also accepted). The \fBexports\fR field uses conditional
exports; the \fBmbjs\fR condition is matched first, then \fBimport\fR, then
\fBdefault\fR:
.PP
.EX
{
  "name": "@scope/pkg",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "mbjs": "./dist/mbjs/index.js",
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
      "default": "./dist/index.js"
    },
    "./feature": "./dist/feature.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "foo": "^1.2.0"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "test": "mbjs test.js"
  }
}
.EE

.SH STORE LAYOUT
.PP
.EX
$TAKEGAWA_HOME/
  store/<sha256>/          extracted package tree (content addressed)
  cache/                   downloaded archives
  registry/<name>/<ver>/   local registry
  global/takegawa_modules/ globally installed packages
  bin/                     shims for packages that declare "bin"
  index.json               name -> version -> integrity index
.EE

.SH BINARIES
When an installed package declares a \fBbin\fR field (a string or an object
mapping command names to scripts), \fBtakegawa\fR writes a small executable
shim into \fBtakegawa_modules/.bin\fR (project installs) or
\fB$TAKEGAWA_HOME/bin\fR (global installs). The shim invokes the target
script with \fBmbjs\fR, so package CLIs run through the normal runtime. Use
\fBtakegawa exec\fR to run them with the bin directories already on
\fBPATH\fR.

.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBtakegawa\fR exits with 0 on success and non-zero on failure.

.SH SEE ALSO
mbjs(1), mbjsc(1)
